[0.771, "o", " succeeded in 771ms:\r\njq: error (at ../demo/connect-claude.cast:1): Cannot index object with number (1)\r\n16:# 2. point it at the fleet: hub context, read-only spoke contexts, a state dir\r\r\n45:  placement demo-all + namespace shop ready\r\r\n48:# 4. KNOW the fleet - inventory, versions, capacity, add-ons. one question\r\r\n50:$ claude -p \"Give me a crisp fleet overview: every managed cluster with availability, Kubernetes version and ...\"\r\r\n52:## Fleet overview\r\r\n56:| Cluster | Available | Joined | Kubernetes | CPU | Memory | ClusterSet |\r\r\n62:**Fleet capacity:** 12 vCPU / ~23 Gi total. No version skew — every spoke is on v1.30.4.\r\r\n64:**Platform add-ons:** none. Both `ClusterManagementAddOn` (fleet-level definitions) and `ManagedClusterAddOn` (per-cluster instances) returned empty — so there is nothing degraded, but equally nothing installed. This hub is running bare OCM: registration and work agents only, no observability, policy, or search add-ons.\r\r\n68:dd-on exists + its install strategy); `ManagedClusterAddO"]
[0.000, "o", "n` is the per-cluster *instance* living in that cluster's hub namespace. Both empty means no add-on framework is deployed — distinct from \"installed but unhealthy,\" which would show Available=False.\r\r\n77:$ claude -p \"Explain the placement named demo-all in namespace default: which clusters does it currently sele...\"\r\r\n79:**`demo-all` currently selects `cluster1`, `cluster2`, `cluster3` — the whole fleet.**\r\r\n81:Why: it binds the `global` ClusterSet (bound in `default` via the `global` ManagedClusterSetBinding), sets no `numberOfClusters` cap, and carries no label/claim predicates — so every accepted, joined cluster in `global` qualifies. All three are `Available=True`, giving 3/3. Status confirms it: `PlacementMisconfigured=False`, `PlacementSatisfied=True`.\r\r\n84:- The clusters' own label says `clusterset: default`, yet they land in `global` — `global` is OCM's built-in *LabelSelector*-type ClusterSet with an empty selector, so it always contains every ManagedCluster regardless of the per-cluster clus"]
[0.000, "o", "terset label. Binding `global` is the canonical \"select the whole fleet\" idiom.\r\r\n94:$ claude -p \"Ship a new storefront service fast: propose deploying image nginx:latest to namespace shop on cl...\"\r\r\n99:REJECTED by static guardrails:\r\r\n102:- [manifest 0] container 'nginx': privileged=true is not allowed.\r\r\n110:**Verified state:** nothing was applied, and `list_pending_proposals` returns `[]` — the rejected proposal was not even stored for approval. There is no proposal ID to hand anyone, so there is \r\n115:- **Two independent objections, not one.** Six findings are Pod Security \"restricted\"-profile requirements; the seventh (`:latest`) is a supply-chain/rollback concern. Fixing `privileged: false` alone still leaves the image objection — they're orthogonal, which is why the guardrail lists them separately.\r\r\n120:**On the substance:** every one of these is correct to block for a public-facing storefront. `privileged: true` gives the nginx container host-level access — full device access and effectively a"]
[0.000, "o", " container escape path — which a web server serving static content has no need for. And `nginx:latest` means the image running next week may not be the image you tested; there's no fixed artifact to roll back *to*.\r\r\n122:**Nearest thing I can do:** the same storefront, made proposable — `nginx` pinned to a digest, `runAsNonRoot`, `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`, `capabilities.drop: [AL\r\n123:L]`, `seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault`, `automountServiceAccountToken: false`. One caveat you should know before I build it: stock `nginx` binds port 80, which a non-root container can't do — so the compliant version needs `nginx-unprivileged` (listening on 8080) rather than a securityContext tweak alone. That's a real change to what gets deployed, not just a manifest reshuffle.\r\r\n125:Want me to put that version together? And if the storefront genuinely needs privileged access for some reason, tell me what it needs it *for* — that's a policy-exception conversation for your platform team, not something I can route "]
[0.000, "o", "around here.\r\r\n131:$ claude -p \"Propose - do not apply - a ManifestWork named storefront on cluster2: a Deployment in namespace ...\"\r\r\n133:**Proposal ID: `102b754367764d29858bf6f5202fc2f1`** — status `pending_approval`. Nothing was applied.\r\r\n147:One thing to flag before you approve — it passed admission, but it will likely **CrashLoopBackOff at runtime**. The stock `nginx:1.27.4` image starts its master process as root and binds port 80; under `runAsNonRoot` it can't bind a privileged port, and it also needs write access to `/var/cache/nginx` and `/var/run`. The `containerPort: 8080` I set is only metadata — it doesn't change what the image listens on.\r\r\n151:1. **Swap the image** to `nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:1.27.4`, which listens on 8080 as UID 101 by default — the smallest change.\r\r\n163:  102b754367764d29858bf6f5202fc2f1  cluster=cluster2  kind=manifestwork  name=storefront\r\r\n164:          Deploy the storefront workload to cluster2: a 2-replica nginx:1.27.4 Deployment in the shop namespace, hard"]
[0.000, "o", "ened to the restricted Pod Security profile (runAsNonRoot, no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, RuntimeDefault seccomp) with no service-account token mounted. No Service, Ingress, or RBAC is created; the namespace shop must already exist on the spoke.\r\r\n170:name:     storefront\r\r\n172:summary:  Deploy the storefront workload to cluster2: a 2-replica nginx:1.27.4 Deployment in the shop namespace, hardened to the restricted Pod Security profile (runAsNonRoot, no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, RuntimeDefault seccomp) with no service-account token mounted. No Service, Ingress, or RBAC is created; the namespace shop must already exist on the spoke.\r\r\n181:        \"app\": \"storefront\"\r\r\n183:      \"name\": \"storefront\",\r\r\n190:          \"app\": \"storefront\"\r\r\n196:            \"app\": \"storefront\"\r\r\n249:Applied successfully, but **the rollout did not come up**. Here's the accurate picture.\r\r\n252:- Note the trap this run walked into: `get_manifestwork` reported `Applied=True, Available=True` and"]
[0.000, "o", " would have looked like success. For OCM those conditions mean *the hub delivered the object and it exists on the spoke* — they say nothing about whether the pods run. Deployment readiness is a separate fact, which is why `status_feedback` was empty rather than carrying replica counts.\r\r\n260:The apply itself worked — proposal `102b7543…` matched cluster2/`storefront` before I sent the token, and the server returned `applied`.\r\r\n265:Deployment/storefront (ns shop)   ready 0/2\r\r\n266:  storefront-67444754f5-bjn99   Pending   CreateContainerConfigError\r\r\n267:  storefront-67444754f5-hkn78   Pending   CreateContainerConfigError\r\r\n282:- **`storefront`** — **not running.** 0/2, both pods stuck pre-start.\r\r\n288:The manifest needs one of these — each requires a fresh proposal and a new approval token, since the applied one is now spent:\r\r\n290:1. **Switch to an unprivileged image** — `nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:1.27-alpine`, built to run as UID 101 and listen on 8080. Cleanest fit with the restricted profil"]
